<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>

<section xml:id="sec_cargo_sorting_pipeline" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Cargo Sorting: Perception-Driven Classification and Placement</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>The cargo-sorting lab asks a robot to pick up objects such as barrels or scoring cubes and deliver each one to a bin that matches its type. Unlike soccer, the emphasis shifts from fast reactive motion to accurate classification and repeatable placement.</p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_cargo_classification_step">
    <title>Classify Before You Move</title>
    <p>Before the robot commits to a pickup, the vision system classifies the cargo item, for example by color or shape, using the same feature-based reasoning introduced in the machine learning chapter. A wrong classification early in the pipeline cannot be fixed by careful driving later, so sorting accuracy depends on getting this first step right.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_cargo_routing_logic">
    <title>Routing Decisions</title>
    <p>Once an item is classified, a simple decision table maps each category to a destination bin:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Category A cargo:</em> route to the near-side bin.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Category B cargo:</em> route to the far-side bin.</p></li>
      <li><p><em class="emphasis">Unclassified or low-confidence cargo:</em> route to a holding zone rather than guessing, since an incorrect placement can cost more than a delayed one.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>This routing logic reuses the path-planning and kinematics work from earlier chapters: once a destination bin is chosen, the robot needs a collision-free path and accurate wheel commands to get there.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec_cargo_repeatability">
    <title>Why Repeatability Matters</title>
    <p>A soccer robot can recover from an imperfect kick by chasing the ball again. A sorting robot that drops cargo in the wrong bin, or fails to release it cleanly, may not get an easy second chance. This makes precise approach alignment and consistent actuator timing especially important in this lab.</p>
  </subsection>
</section>
